13:41:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, rooftop solar panels also generate power in diffuse light, not just direct sun—they just generate a lot less. On a cloudy day, a rooftop system might produce 10-25% of its rated output rather than dropping to zero. That's why solar installers still recommend panels in climates that are often overcast, and why production estimates account for cloudy days rather than assuming zero output. So the underlying physics is the same for both: photovoltaic cells respond to any light, with output scaling with intensity. Direct sun = strongest output. Diffuse/indirect light = weaker output. The reason a calculator behaves differently in practice comes down to **scale of power needed**, not some difference in how the technology fundamentally works: - A rooftop system needs to produce meaningful power output—kilowatts—to be useful for running your house. Weak diffuse light might only produce a small fraction of that, which is why cloudy days noticeably matter for total daily energy production. - A calculator needs practically nothing—millionths of a watt. Even the weak trickle of energy from indoor ambient light easily clears that threshold. Same principle, wildly different power requirements, so the practical "does it work or not" experience diverges a lot. It's not that calculators use different physics—it's that their tiny power draw makes the weak signal from indirect light "good enough," whereas a house needs the strong signal from direct sun to actually produce meaningful electricity.
